---
import InfoLayout from '../layouts/InfoLayout.astro';
import InfoSection from '../components/InfoSection.astro';
import SpecTable from '../components/SpecTable.astro';
---

<InfoLayout
  path="/sample-kit/"
  title="Sample Kit | Material Swatches and Test Boxes"
  description="Request material swatches, a plain structural sample or a printed proof, and see which of the three answers your question."
  h1="Sample Kit"
  eyebrow="Ordering"
  lead="There are three different things people mean by a sample, and they answer three different questions. Asking for the right one saves a round trip."
  ogTitle="Packaging Samples: Swatches, Structures and Proofs"
  ogDescription="Which type of sample answers which question, and what to test when it arrives."
  points={['Three sample types', 'Test with real product', 'Tell us what you need to verify']}
  ctaSecondaryHref="/how-to-order/"
  ctaSecondaryLabel="See the ordering steps"
  ctaHeading="Tell us what you need to check"
  ctaBody="Say which question you are trying to answer — does it fit, does it feel right, or does the colour work — and we will send the sample that answers it."
>
  <InfoSection
    id="three-types"
    heading="Three kinds of sample"
    intro="Each answers a specific question. Requesting the wrong one is the most common reason a sample turns out not to help."
  >
    <SpecTable
      caption="Sample types and what each one tells you."
      columns={['Sample', 'Answers', 'Does not answer']}
      rows={[
        [
          'Material swatches',
          'How a board or a label stock looks and feels; how holographic film shifts in real light',
          'Whether your product fits, or how your artwork prints',
        ],
        [
          'Plain structural sample',
          'Whether your container fits with the right clearance, how the carton stands and closes, whether the insert holds',
          'Colour, finish or how the print will look',
        ],
        [
          'Printed proof or production sample',
          'Colour on the actual stock, foil and spot UV position, whether small type is legible',
          'Little else — it is the final check before a run',
        ],
      ]}
    />
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="swatches"
    heading="Material swatches"
    intro="Useful early, when you are still deciding between board grades or considering a specialty stock."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      A swatch tells you things no image can. Kraft board is warmer in the hand than
      it looks on a screen. Soft-touch lamination is the difference between a pack
      that feels mid-market and one that feels expensive. Holographic film behaves
      completely differently under store lighting, daylight and a phone camera flash,
      which is why we recommend swatches of rainbow, mirror and pearlescent side by
      side before committing to an
      <a href="/holographic-lip-gloss-boxes/">iridescent specification</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      Tell us which decision you are trying to make and we will send the relevant
      swatches rather than a generic pack of everything.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="structural"
    heading="Plain structural samples"
    intro="The single most valuable sample in a packaging project, and the one most often skipped under time pressure."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      A plain unprinted sample is cut and creased from the approved die line in the
      specified board. It exists to answer one question: does this carton work with
      your product. For a rigid box the equivalent is an assembled white sample,
      which also lets you judge proportion, lid fit and magnet strength.
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      <strong>Test it with your real product, not empty.</strong> A fit that works
      on paper sometimes fails with a slightly pillowed sachet, a lacquered lipstick
      case that measures marginally larger than its drawing, or a paper tube with
      more surface friction than a plastic one. Then check four things:
    </p>
    <ol class="ordered">
      <li>Does the container go in and out without forcing, and without rattling?</li>
      <li>Does the carton close cleanly, and would it do so quickly on your packing line?</li>
      <li>Does it stand upright, and does the base hold when you lift it by the top?</li>
      <li>If there is an insert, does the product stay put when the box is shaken?</li>
    </ol>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="printed"
    heading="Printed proofs and production samples"
    intro="The last check before a run. What you approve here is what production is measured against."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      A calibrated digital proof is enough on plain coated white board with process
      colour. On kraft, metallised, holographic or black-through stock it is not,
      because none of those substrates behaves the way white paper does, and an
      on-screen approval will not predict the printed result.
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      Where the job carries foil, embossing or spot UV, ask for a
      production-standard sample rather than a flat proof. Position and register on
      those finishes need physical checking, and the raised or reflective effect
      cannot be judged from a printed simulation. What each finish does is set out on
      the <a href="/finishes/">coatings page</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Check spot colours under daylight rather than office lighting, read the
      smallest type at final size, and if the pack carries a shade swatch, hold it
      next to the actual product. Then approve in writing and keep the proof.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="how-to-request"
    heading="How to request samples"
    intro="Two things make a sample request genuinely useful."
  >
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>
        <strong>Say what you need to verify.</strong> "Does my tube fit" and "does
        this board feel premium enough" need completely different samples.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Send the container if you have one.</strong> Posting a filled sample
        to us means the structural sample is built around the real thing rather than
        around a nominal dimension, which removes most fit risk in one step.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="measure">
      Sample availability, cost and lead time vary by type and by how far a project
      has progressed, and we will confirm all three in writing when you ask. Use the
      <a href="/request-a-quote/">quote request form</a> and note the sample you
      want in the additional details field, or the short form on any product page.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="what-samples-cannot-do"
    heading="What a sample cannot tell you"
    intro="Worth being realistic about, so a sample is not asked to carry more weight than it can."
  >
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>
        A single sample does not represent production variation. A run has tolerance
        across it, particularly on hand-assembled rigid boxes.
      </li>
      <li>
        A digital proof on white paper does not represent colour on a different
        substrate, however good the proofing device.
      </li>
      <li>
        A sample cannot verify your regulatory compliance. That is your review to
        run, as described in the
        <a href="/artwork-guidelines/">artwork guidelines</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        A structural sample does not test long-term shelf behaviour. If you are
        putting a soft balm in a paper tube, that needs a proper stability test over
        time rather than a fit check.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </InfoSection>
</InfoLayout>
